# A base constructor makes its object in its own body, for its new.target. While every construction through it was answered with that one node, `new Square(2)` and `this` behind super() were the whole family of Shape: a store `this.side = ..` was a store into Rect and Disk too, which got `side` as an appended field, and n classes below one base cost n x n fields.
# A construction whose row of constructors hands back only the object of its construction answers with the instances of the class of its new.target: `new Square(2)` with Square's, a super call that hands its own new.target up the row with those of the classes that arrive there.
# RUN: link fields mechanisms=all
# CHECK: "side"
# RUN(NONE): link fields mechanisms=all
# NONE-NOT: appended
